  (no_connect (at 45.72 274.32) (uuid b22b40c7-0372-5f26-b950-d59038bbfad4))
  (text "FROM THE HAND-WRITTEN NETLIST THIS SHEET REPLACES (verbatim; the part notes are on each part's Note field):\n\nAUTHORITATIVE for connectivity of the CARRIER BOARD ITSELF - the dev board, its connector J-MCU, and the SPI\negress drawn in carrier.md §4. THIS FILE SITS AT hardware/carrier/, not in a circuit directory, because\ncarrier.md is the board page rather than a circuit page: its bom.csv fragment holds the dev board, its ribbon\nand connector, and the PCB, and its drawings show four OTHER circuits' parts for context. THE RIBBON IS NOT A\nCOMPONENT. U-MCU-RT is on the lid and CBL-MCU-RIBBON is soldered to its pad rows and plugged into J-MCU on the\nmain board: one conductor per pad, straight, with no pin map of its own. So each Matrix pin and its J-MCU pin\nare ONE NET here, exactly as interfaces/spi-link treats the umbilical cable. J-MCU's pins are numbered: ribbon\nconductor n is J-MCU pin n, allocated by ADR 0018 (the J-MCU row gives the map). PARALLEL CONDUCTORS ARE\nSEPARATE J-MCU PINS ON ONE NET. Three conductors carry 5V and four carry GND, for current and for the ground\nshift under the ADC's reference (carrier.md, \"The Matrix and the umbilical at the tail end\"). On the Matrix\ntwo of the 5V conductors share the 5V pad, and three of the grounds land on test pad TP3 - so U-MCU-RT.P5V and\nU-MCU-RT.TP2 are both its VCC_5V, and U-MCU-RT.GND and U-MCU-RT.TP3 both its GND. §2's ANALOG FRONT END IS NOT\nHERE. The reference, the buffer, the sensor and the ADC are drawn on this page and owned by\ncarrier/breath-excitation- reference, carrier/breath-adc and interfaces/breath-sense-link. They are declared\n`foreign:` so the drawing stays checkable without this file pretending to own them, and they are netlisted in\ntheir own directories against this page.\n\nto the cluster chain\n\nto the LED strip\n\nto the service header\n\nthe shared SPI host: the ADC on this board, the DAC two metres away\n\nsupply\n\nJ-UMB pin 8 arrives here and is this board's ground (ADR 0018)\n\n--- the shared SPI host, at the MCU pins -------------------------- ONE HOST, TWO DEVICES. The same clock and\ndata reach the MCP3202 on this board and the DAC8568 two metres away; only the chip selects differ. So the MCU\npin and the umbilical conductor are DIFFERENT NETS with the series resistor between them, and the ADC hangs on\nthe MCU side.\n\nLocal: the MCU pin to its series resistor, and nothing else. It crosses no boundary, so it is not in\nhardware/nets.yaml.\n\n--- the cluster chain ---------------------------------------------\n\n--- the LED strip ---------------------------------------------------\n\n--- the service header ----------------------------------------------\n\n--- supply ----------------------------------------------------------\n\nThree conductors: two on the Matrix's 5V pad, one on TP2.\n\nFour conductors: one on the Matrix's GND pad, three on TP3. DIG_GND, J-UMB pin 8 (interfaces/spi-link), ties\nto this board's ground at J-UMB - a short, wide connection at the pin, copper and not a part (ADR 0018). On\nthe board that copper is a net tie, NT-DIG, beside the pin: DIG_GND keeps its name because at the module end\nconductor 8 is its own net, and where it meets AGND_MOD there is dig-gnd-topology, still disputed.\n\n--- reset and boot, to the service header (ADR 0018) ----------------- Pulled up on the Matrix by R8 / R10\n(10k to its 3V3) and pulled down by its buttons. Nothing in series; whatever is on HDR-SERVICE pulls to ground\nonly.\n\n--- the two spare GPIO the ribbon carries ----------------------------- Local: they reach J-MCU and nothing\nelse on this board (two test pads beside it, per the J-MCU row).\n\nThe fourth clamp channel has no fourth signal: J-UMB pin 8 is DIG_GND, which is a return rather than something\nto clamp." (at 256.54 38.099999999999994 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left top)) (uuid f92b740f-c70f-567b-a8a1-f38d9952ec5c))
  (symbol (lib_id "Device:R") (at 116.84 233.68 0) (unit 1)
    (in_bom yes) (on_board yes) (dnp no)
    (uuid 65bc28b9-12c0-44b8-af6f-097be6bd8040)
    (property "Reference" "R-CS-PULL-INST" (at 119.38 232.41 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left)))
    (property "Value" "10k 1%" (at 119.38 234.95 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) (justify left)))
    (property "Footprint" "Resistor_SMD:R_0805_2012Metric" (at 116.84 233.68 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide))
    (property "Datasheet" "" (at 116.84 233.68 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide))
    (property "Row" "R-CS-PULL-INST" (at 116.84 233.68 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide))
    (property "Pins" "1=1 2=2" (at 116.84 233.68 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide))
    (property "Note" "CS_MOD's idle pull-up, AT THE INSTRUMENT END (owner, 2026-09-30: 'Fix the SPI pull up'): IO34 to the dev board's 3V3, on the MCU side of R-SPI-SER-CS, beside J-MCU pins 13 (3V3) and 14 (IO34). IO34 has no pull of its own at reset - 'IE' only [ds ESP32-S3-datasheet-v2.2.pdf p.17] - so this is what holds CS deselected while the ESP32 resets and boots. It is not a strapping pin (GPIO0/3/45/46, p.26). Values and the three link states: interfaces/spi-link/spi-link.md, 'CS_MOD's two pulls'" (at 116.84 233.68 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide))
    (property "Pins_source" "a two-terminal resistor" (at 116.84 233.68 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide))
    (property "Manufacturer" "UNI-ROYAL(Uniroyal Elec)" (at 116.84 233.68 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide))
    (property "MPN" "0805W8F1002T5E" (at 116.84 233.68 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide))
    (property "LCSC" "C17414" (at 116.84 233.68 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide))
    (property "Assembly" "machine" (at 116.84 233.68 0) (effects (font (size 1.27 1.27)) hide))
    (pin "1" (uuid f25e4715-820a-47d3-b945-1001a1989fc7))
    (pin "2" (uuid 466dc469-e9fc-485e-aaac-70d41b2fab11))
